To investigate the evolution of the nanobainitic microstructure during tempering at temperatures ranging from 150°C to 600°C for 2 h, the results obtained from dilatometry, synchrotron X-ray radiation, X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis, and scanning electron microscopy were correlated. Based on these analyses, four main stages of indirect microstructural decomposition were identified. The first stage occurred within the temperature range of 150–360°C and was characterized by the detection of several key microstructural features. Most notably, ε-carbide precipitation from bainitic ferrite laths was observed through synchrotron X-ray (Fig.  5 ). One of the primary constituents of the initial nanobainitic microstructure obtained via low-temperature isothermal transformation is bainitic ferrite. As reported by F. G. Caballero et al. [ 37 ] and C. Garcia-Mateo et al. [ 38 ] bainitic ferrite formed under such conditions has a high dislocation density and is supersaturated with carbon. Additionally, significant carbon segregation occurs along dislocations, forming Cottrell atmospheres during isothermal holding. This phenomenon supports the conclusion that carbon atoms trapped at crystal defects in bainitic ferrite become thermally activated at temperatures between 150°C and 360°C, resulting in ε-carbide precipitation within the bainitic ferrite laths [ 39 ]. This behavior has also been confirmed in other studies [ 40 ,  41 – 42 ]. Furthermore, X-ray analysis revealed that the carbon content of bainitic ferrite decreases with increasing tempering temperature compared to the untempered state. In this temperature range, partial decomposition of retained austenite was identified simultaneously. A nanobainitic microstructure consists of thin films and blocky of retained austenite. Due to its lower carbon content, blocky retained austenite is thermodynamically less stable than thin films [ 43 ]. Additionally, the carbon in blocky austenite may be distributed heterogeneously, further reducing its thermal stability [ 2 ].Therefore, it is reasonable to suggest that blocky retained austenite decomposes into bainitic ferrite during tempering [ 29 ]. Evidence for this transformation was found. First, positive dilatation was recorded during tempering at 210°C (Fig.  4 b) [ 44 ], a phenomenon characteristic of bainitic transformation. Moreover, excess carbon is expelled and diffuses into the surrounding austenite during this transformation, leading to its local enrichment and stabilization [ 26 ,  45 ]. Therefore, no martensitic transformation was detected during cooling after tempering. Concurrently, a decrease in the overall amount of retained austenite was observed with increasing tempering temperature. This decrease is attributed to the bainitic transformation of retained austenite, as demonstrated in Fig.  6 . At this stage, the microstructure consisted of carbon-depleted bainitic ferrite  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
