Several microstructural transformations were identified in the second stage, which occurred at 450°C. These include the evolution of ε-carbides into cementite, the precipitation of cementite at the interfaces of bainitic ferrite and retained austenite, and the transformation of retained austenite into martensite when cooled after tempering. At this temperature, the diffusivity of carbon in retained austenite increases significantly and plays a crucial role in driving diffusion-controlled transformations, as indicated by H. S. Park et al. [ 46 ]. This effect is likely due to facilitated carbon diffusion from bainitic ferrite laths and retained austenite. Consequently, cementite precipitates preferentially along bainitic ferrite/retained austenite boundaries [ 47 ]. At this stage, the newly formed carbides are primarily cementite, replacing the transitional ε-carbides formed previously [ 48 ]. The evolution of ε-carbides and the concurrent precipitation of cementite were confirmed by synchrotron X-ray diffraction (Fig.  5 ), with specific reflections visible in the enlarged 2θ range (Fig.  5 b). This was also confirmed by dilatometric analysis (Fig.  4 ), which recorded a contraction in sample length after 2 h of tempering at 450°C [ 49 ]. A reduction in carbon content within both bainitic ferrite and retained austenite was also observed at this stage (Fig.  6 ), which further confirms the increased diffusivity of carbon within these phases. The precipitation of cementite from carbon-supersaturated bainitic ferrite gradually transforms its lattice from a body-centered tetragonal (BCT) structure to an equilibrium body-centered cubic (BCC) state [ 20 ]. Additionally, enhanced carbon diffusion leads to carbon depletion in retained austenite, reducing its thermal stability and enabling fresh martensite formation during final cooling [ 26 ,  28 ]. This behavior was observed in specimens tempered at 450°C, 500°C, and 600°C. An increase in the martensite start temperature was recorded with increasing tempering temperature through dilatometric analysis in Fig.  4 . The presence of M/γ R  islands was also confirmed by microstructural observations (Fig.  7 ). After tempering at 450 °C, the microstructure was composed of  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
